## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a compliance feature into a concrete, time-bound cost-benefit analysis suitable for a beginner. It tests how assumptions drive ROI and how to articulate go/no-go criteria.

## Key Concepts

- Cost estimation basics for small features
- ROI calculation and break-even timing
- Risk penalties avoided and adoption uplift
- Simple, testable calculations and trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function breakEvenMonths(laborRatePerHr, weeks, engineers, monthlyOverhead, annualBenefit) {
  const upfront = laborRatePerHr * weeks * engineers * 40;
  const monthlyBenefit = annualBenefit / 12;
  const netMonthlyBenefit = monthlyBenefit - monthlyOverhead;
  if (netMonthlyBenefit <= 0) return Infinity;
  return Math.ceil(upfront / netMonthlyBenefit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust for ARR vs one-time benefits?
- What metrics would you track after launch to validate forecasts?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to reason about localized data processing, privacy controls, and cost trade-offs for a beginner-level feature.

## Key Concepts

- On-device data processing and storage budgeting
- Delta synchronization and telemetry cost control
- Privacy by design and opt-in controls
- Staged rollout with measurable go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
function deltaSize(prev, curr) {
  const delta = {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(curr)) {
    delta[k] = Math.max(0, (curr[k] || 0) - (prev[k] || 0));
  }
  return delta;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle data conflicts when multiple devices sync simultaneously?
- What fallback mechanisms would you implement if delta sync fails?
- How would you optimize the storage algorithm to handle bursty data patterns?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model a device-resident telemetry system that serves multiple apps while enforcing data minimization and consent, balancing privacy, UX impact, and backend savings.

## Key Concepts

- Data deduplication and cross-app coordination on-device
- Consent lifecycle and policy enforcement across tenants
- On-device aggregation, sampling, and energy budgeting
- Privacy/compliance costs and drift monitoring
- Rollout strategy, break-even analysis, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
function aggregateDeltas(deltas) {
  // de-duplicate by user/app+event, then sample
  const seen = new Set();
  const out = [];
  for (const d of deltas) {
    const key = d.userId + '|' + d.appId + '|' + d.eventId;
    if (!seen.has(key)) {
      seen.add(key);
      if (Math.random() < d.sampleRate) out.push(d.aggregate);
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify cross-app dedup cost vs backend savings?
- What would a staged rollout look like to minimize risk and ensure compliance?

